artment of Normandy, dominated by a castle on a rocky outcrop above the Ante valley.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/falak-ol-aflak"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/falak-ol-aflak.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Falak-ol-Aflak</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">Falak-ol-Aflak is a castle on a hill in the centre of Khorramabad in Lorestan province, western Iran.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/falaki-shirvani"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/falaki-shirvani.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Falaki Shirvani</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Falaki Shirvani was a Persian poet of the twelfth century from Shirvan, in present-day Azerbaijan.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/faleme-river"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/faleme-river.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Falémé River</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">The Falémé is a river of West Africa, a left-bank tributary of the Senegal, that rises in the Fouta Djallon of Guinea and forms much of the border between Senegal and…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/falerii-novi"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/falerii-novi.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Falerii Novi</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Falerii Novi was the new town founded on open ground after Rome destroyed the Faliscan hill city of Falerii in 241 BCE and moved its inhabitants there.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/falkland-islands-company"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/falkland-islands-company.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Falkland Islands Company</span><span class="enc-type">Institution</span><span class="enc-snip">The Falkland Islands Company is a British trading company incorporated by royal charter in 1851 to develop the Falkland Islands.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/falklands-war"><span class="enc-name">Falklands War</span><span class="enc-type">War · 1982</span><span class="enc-snip">The Falklands War was a ten-week conflict in 1982 between Argentina and the United Kingdom over the Falkland Islands and South Georgia.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/fall-of-acre-1291"><span class="enc-name">Fall of Acre (1291)</span><span class="enc-type">Siege · 1291</span><span class="enc-snip">The fall of Acre in 1291 was the capture of the last major Crusader stronghold in the Levant by the Mamluk Sultanate.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/fall-of-beijing-1644"><span class="enc-name">Fall of Beijing (1644)</span><span class="enc-type">Siege · 1644</span><span class="enc-snip">The fall of Beijing in 1644 was the capture of the Ming capital by the rebel army of Li Zicheng.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/fall-of-constantinople"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/fall-of-constantinople.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Fall of Constantinople</span><span class="enc-type">Siege · 1453</span><span class="enc-snip">The fall of Constantinople was the capture of the Byzantine capital by the Ottoman sultan Mehmed II in 1453, after a siege in which heavy artillery played a major role.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/fall-of-cordoba"><span class="enc-name">Fall of Córdoba</span><span class="enc-type">Siege · 1236</span><span class="enc-snip">The fall of Córdoba was the capture of the city by Ferdinand III of Castile in 1236.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/fall-of-granada"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/fall-of-granada.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Fall of Granada</span><span class="enc-type">Siege · 1492</span><span class="enc-snip">The fall of Granada was the surrender of the Nasrid kingdom of Granada, the last Muslim state in Iberia, to Ferdinand and Isabella in 1492.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/fall-of-harran"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/fall-of-harran.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Fall of Harran</span><span class="enc-type">Siege · 609 BCE</span><span class="enc-snip">The fall of Harran in 609 BCE was the defeat of the last Assyrian remnant, under Ashur-uballit II, by the Babylonians and their allies.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/fall-of-herat-1507"><span class="enc-name">Fall of Herat (1507)</span><span class="enc-type">Siege · 1507</span><span class="enc-snip">The fall of Herat in 1507 was the capture of the last Timurid capital by the Uzbek leader Muhammad Shaybani Khan.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/fall-of-nineveh"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/fall-of-nineveh.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Fall of Nineveh</span><span class="enc-type">Siege · 612 BCE</span><span class="enc-snip">The fall of Nineveh was the destruction of the Assyrian capital in 612 BCE by a coalition of Medes and Babylonians.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/fall-of-oyo-ile"><span class="enc-name">Fall of Oyo-Ile</span><span class="enc-type">Event</span><span class="enc-snip">The fall of Oyo-Ile, around 1835–1836, was the abandonment of the capital of the Oyo Empire under pressure from the Ilorin emirate and the Sokoto Caliphate's expansion.<